The first display value is a fallback: a browser without two-keyword display keeps today's behavior. The second rule stops nested lists gaining 6.5px, because a box no longer lets its last child's margin collapse through it.

design:llm-block.rationale:
The AI block has no border or background. Visually it is a run of paragraphs with a small label, so the natural rule is the paragraph rule: flow beside the widget. That is the simplest choice and leaves the author free to place widgets top or bottom, on either side. The harness and screenshots showed that today's flow is already right in the common cases (left and right, 33% and 50%, short and long, lead-in paragraph). They also showed four real defects, all fixed without changing that flow:
- the label shoved to the widget's edge after a bottom-aligned left widget;
- the label's rule cutting through a left widget when the block starts with a list;
- the post's widget being unclickable beside AI text;
- a nested widget leaking out of the AI block into author text, which matters for attribution.
Box beside wastes space under the widget for long AI answers and has no border to protect. Clears below punches holes and causes a runaway feedback loop with bottom alignment. The final CSS gives 0 text or box overlaps, 0 blocked hit points and no horizontal scroll at all 15 widths in both views. Editor and Post tab match, apart from two editor/reader spacing mismatches the CSS does not cause (see openIssues).
